<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josif Rajačić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1785">1785</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josif Rajačić was a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churchman, metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlovci</a> from 1842. At the May Assembly of Sremski Karlovci in 1848 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> elected him patriarch and proclaimed the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>. He sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> against the Hungarian revolution and later served as administrator of the Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeschwar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josif-rajacic.jpg" alt="Josif Rajačić" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Kriehuber (1800-1876) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josef_Rajacic_Litho_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Josif Rajačić was born on 20 July 1785 in Lučani, a former village near Brinje in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lika">Lika</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. He studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlovci</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, but left his studies in 1809 to join the Imperial Austrian Army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a>. On 10 April 1810 he became a monk of the Serbian Orthodox Church at Gomirje Monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Rajačić became Eparch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> on 24 June 1829 and Eparch of Vršac on 5 July 1833, and in August 1842 he was named Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlovci</a>. As metropolitan he supported the education of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>: many new Serbian schools were opened, and he founded the Patriarchal Library and Print Works.</p><p>At the May Assembly of 1848 he was proclaimed Patriarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> from the balcony of the town hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stevan-supljikac">Stevan Šupljikac</a> was chosen as the first Voivode of Serbian Vojvodina. Besides his spiritual office he shared the political and military leadership of the Serbs in the war that followed, serving as administrator of Serbian Vojvodina and head of its new government, the praviteljstvo. On 5 June 1848 the inauguration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-jelacic">Josip Jelačić</a> as Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> took place in the Patriarch's presence, as Juraj Haulik was absent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Promised autonomy, Rajačić allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution of 1848</a>, and after the Hungarians were defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> made him civil commissioner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>. He spent much of his energy trying to bring Vojvodina under Serbian administration. He was made a baron of the Austrian Empire and received the Order of Leopold and the Order of the Iron Crown of the first class. In April 1861 he co-presided over the Annunciation Council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a>, and he died on 1 December 1861.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josif Rajačić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A national assembly at Sremski Karlovci in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian Vojvodina in Srem, Baranya, Bačka and Banat, elected <b>Josif Rajačić</b> patriarch and Colonel Stevan Šupljikac voivode, and set up a Main Board led by Đorđe Stratimirović.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brinje, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45,15.13&amp;year=1834" aria-label="Brinje on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Josif Rajačić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4111 6168h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4111 6168h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45,15.13&amp;year=1834">What was here in 1834? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
